{"version_id": 43, "article_id": 5, "level_code": "en_b1", "lang": "en", "level": 3, "level_label": "B1 中级", "title": "Oil Companies Ask Supreme Court to Stop Climate Lawsuits", "title_zh": "石油公司想让最高法院叫停气候诉讼", "topic": "tech", "source": "arstechnica", "source_url": "https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/big-oil-asks-supreme-court-to-kill-climate-lawsuits-before-trial/", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://live.staticflickr.com/3913/14334624106_a9bcc306a9_b.jpg", "credit": "Bernard Spragg · cc0 1.0 · via flickr"}, "published_date": "2026-10-06", "word_count": 245, "reading_minutes": 1.2, "lead": "Oil companies went to the US Supreme Court on Monday. They want the court to block more than two dozen climate lawsuits before any trial begins.", "lead_zh": "周一，石油公司把官司打到了美国最高法院。他们想赶在开庭之前，就让二十多起气候诉讼停下来。", "preview": "The US Supreme Court heard arguments from oil companies on Monday. The case could decide the future of more than two dozen lawsuits. These l", "preview_zh": "周一，美国最高法院听取了石油公司的辩论。这起案件可能决定二十多起诉讼的命运。这些诉讼称，石油行业在气候变化问题上欺骗了公众，并要求赔偿数十亿美元，用来支付极端天气造成的损失。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 31, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@a79b424/content/audio/5/en_b1.mp3", "duration": 89.912, "size_bytes": 543168, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 269, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@a79b424/content/data/versions/43.json", "paragraphs": ["The US Supreme Court heard arguments from oil companies on Monday. The case could decide the future of more than two dozen lawsuits. These lawsuits say the industry tricked the public about climate change, and they ask for billions of dollars to pay for damage from extreme weather.", "The case began in 2018, when the city and county of Boulder sued ExxonMobil and Suncor Energy in Colorado state court. The oil companies now argue that federal law should block state and local claims like this. Their lawyer said Boulder is trying to use state law to control oil company activity around the world.", "However, Boulder's lawyer disagreed. He said the case is not about emissions, but about deception. Boulder is asking for money, not for any limit on oil company activities. Because the case has already survived many appeals, the companies want the Supreme Court to stop it now.", "The first question was whether the court can hear the case at this stage. Some justices suggested it might be too early to step in. Justice Samuel Alito did not attend the hearing because he owns stock in oil companies, and his absence could lead to a tie vote.", "Many states and local governments have paused their own cases while they wait for this decision. The court is expected to rule by the end of its term in June. Although the result is uncertain, it could either stop the claims quickly or finally let trials begin."], "paragraphs_zh": ["周一，美国最高法院听取了石油公司的辩论。这起案件可能决定二十多起诉讼的命运。这些诉讼称，石油行业在气候变化问题上欺骗了公众，并要求赔偿数十亿美元，用来支付极端天气造成的损失。", "案件始于2018年，当时博尔德市和县在科罗拉多州法院起诉了埃克森美孚和森科能源。如今，石油公司辩称，联邦法律应当阻止州和地方提出这类索赔。他们的律师说，博尔德是想用州法律来管石油公司在全世界的活动。", "不过，博尔德的律师不同意。他说，这案子争的不是排放，而是欺骗。博尔德要的是钱，不是限制石油公司的任何活动。由于这起案件已经挺过了多次上诉，石油公司希望最高法院现在就叫停它。", "第一个问题是，最高法院在这个阶段能不能审这案子。一些大法官认为，现在介入可能还太早。大法官塞缪尔·阿利托没有出席听证会，因为他持有石油公司的股票，而他的缺席可能导致投票出现平局。", "许多州和地方政府已经暂停了自己的案件，等待这一裁决。最高法院预计将在6月任期结束前作出判决。虽然结果还不确定，但它要么很快终止这些索赔，要么终于让审判开始。"], "body": "The US Supreme Court heard arguments from oil companies on Monday. The case could decide the future of more than two dozen lawsuits. These lawsuits say the industry tricked the public about climate change, and they ask for billions of dollars to pay for damage from extreme weather.\n\nThe case began in 2018, when the city and county of Boulder sued ExxonMobil and Suncor Energy in Colorado state court. The oil companies now argue that federal law should block state and local claims like this. Their lawyer said Boulder is trying to use state law to control oil company activity around the world.\n\nHowever, Boulder's lawyer disagreed. He said the case is not about emissions, but about deception. Boulder is asking for money, not for any limit on oil company activities. Because the case has already survived many appeals, the companies want the Supreme Court to stop it now.\n\nThe first question was whether the court can hear the case at this stage. Some justices suggested it might be too early to step in. Justice Samuel Alito did not attend the hearing because he owns stock in oil companies, and his absence could lead to a tie vote.\n\nMany states and local governments have paused their own cases while they wait for this decision. The court is expected to rule by the end of its term in June. Although the result is uncertain, it could either stop the claims quickly or finally let trials begin.", "body_zh": "周一，美国最高法院听取了石油公司的辩论。这起案件可能决定二十多起诉讼的命运。这些诉讼称，石油行业在气候变化问题上欺骗了公众，并要求赔偿数十亿美元，用来支付极端天气造成的损失。\n\n案件始于2018年，当时博尔德市和县在科罗拉多州法院起诉了埃克森美孚和森科能源。如今，石油公司辩称，联邦法律应当阻止州和地方提出这类索赔。他们的律师说，博尔德是想用州法律来管石油公司在全世界的活动。\n\n不过，博尔德的律师不同意。他说，这案子争的不是排放，而是欺骗。博尔德要的是钱，不是限制石油公司的任何活动。由于这起案件已经挺过了多次上诉，石油公司希望最高法院现在就叫停它。\n\n第一个问题是，最高法院在这个阶段能不能审这案子。一些大法官认为，现在介入可能还太早。大法官塞缪尔·阿利托没有出席听证会，因为他持有石油公司的股票，而他的缺席可能导致投票出现平局。\n\n许多州和地方政府已经暂停了自己的案件，等待这一裁决。最高法院预计将在6月任期结束前作出判决。虽然结果还不确定，但它要么很快终止这些索赔，要么终于让审判开始。", "text": "The US Supreme Court heard arguments from oil companies on Monday. The case could decide the future of more than two dozen lawsuits. These lawsuits say the industry tricked the public about climate change, and they ask for billions of dollars to pay for damage from extreme weather. The case began in 2018, when the city and county of Boulder sued ExxonMobil and Suncor Energy in Colorado state court. The oil companies now argue that federal law should block state and local claims like this. Their lawyer said Boulder is trying to use state law to control oil company activity around the world. However, Boulder's lawyer disagreed. He said the case is not about emissions, but about deception. Boulder is asking for money, not for any limit on oil company activities. Because the case has already survived many appeals, the companies want the Supreme Court to stop it now. The first question was whether the court can hear the case at this stage. Some justices suggested it might be too early to step in. Justice Samuel Alito did not attend the hearing because he owns stock in oil companies, and his absence could lead to a tie vote. Many states and local governments have paused their own cases while they wait for this decision. The court is expected to rule by the end of its term in June. Although the result is uncertain, it could either stop the claims quickly or finally let trials begin.", "vocab": [{"word": "lawsuit", "pos": "noun", "zh": "诉讼", "note": "常用搭配：file a lawsuit（提起诉讼）"}, {"word": "Supreme Court", "pos": "noun", "zh": "最高法院", "note": "美国最高级别的法院"}, {"word": "argue", "pos": "verb", "zh": "辩论；主张", "note": "法律语境中常指“在法庭上辩论”"}, {"word": "block", "pos": "verb", "zh": "阻止；阻挡", "note": "比 stop 更正式"}, {"word": "claim", "pos": "noun", "zh": "索赔；主张", "note": "法律中可指“索赔要求”"}, {"word": "deception", "pos": "noun", "zh": "欺骗", "note": "动词是 deceive"}, {"word": "emissions", "pos": "noun", "zh": "排放（物）", "note": "常指温室气体排放"}, {"word": "preempt", "pos": "verb", "zh": "抢先；优先于", "note": "法律中常指联邦法优先于州法"}, {"word": "jurisdiction", "pos": "noun", "zh": "管辖权", "note": "法院是否有权审理案件"}, {"word": "tie vote", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "平局投票", "note": "票数相同，无法分出胜负"}], "title_original": "Big Oil asks Supreme Court to kill climate lawsuits before trial", "summary_original": "Oil companies say federal law should block state climate deception claims.", "body_original": "Avoiding a reckoning\nBig Oil asks Supreme Court to kill climate lawsuits before trial\nOil companies say federal law should block state climate deception claims.\nNicholas Kusnetz, Inside Climate News\n–\nOct 6, 2026 9:29 am\n|\n52\nPeople protest in front of the Supreme Court during arguments in the Suncor v. Boulder case on Monday in Washington, D.C.\nCredit:\nJim Watson/AFP via Getty Images\nPeople protest in front of the Supreme Court during arguments in the Suncor v. Boulder case on Monday in Washington, D.C.\nCredit:\nJim Watson/AFP via Getty Images\nText\nsettings\nStory text\nSize\nSmall\nStandard\nLarge\nWidth\n*\nStandard\nWide\nLinks\nStandard\nOrange\n* Subscribers only\nLearn more\nMinimize to nav\nThe US Supreme Court heard arguments from oil companies on Monday that could help decide the fate of more than two dozen lawsuits accusing the industry of deceiving the public about climate change. The claims seek potentially billions of dollars in damages to help pay the costs imposed by climate-related extreme weather.\nYet the questions before the court did not focus on the merits of these claims but instead on technical legal matters over whether they should proceed at all.\nThe case being argued was brought in 2018 by the city and county of Boulder against ExxonMobil and Suncor Energy in Colorado state court. While the case and dozens like it have survived multiple appeals in state and federal courts, the oil companies are asking the Supreme Court to declare that federal law preempts state and local governments from bringing such claims.\n“This case represents an unprecedented effort to use state law to regulate global conduct,” said Kannon Shanmugam, arguing for Exxon and Suncor. In effect, Shanmugam said, Boulder is attempting to use state law to regulate oil company activity around the world. “If Boulder’s claims are allowed to go forward, some 90,000 municipalities across the country would have the ability to make national and international energy policy by asking juries to impose catastrophic damages on selected fossil fuel producers.”\nBut Boulder and dozens of other plaintiffs have argued their claims do not attempt to impose any limits on oil companies at all—the Boulder case seeks damages, not an injunction on any activity.\n“This case is not about emissions,” said Kevin Russell, who argued the case for Boulder. “It’s about deception.”\nOil companies and conservative groups have been ramping up a campaign to block the lawsuits from proceeding as they inch closer to trial. Some states have enacted laws barring such lawsuits, and Republicans have introduced federal legislation that would shield oil companies from climate liability claims. The Trump administration has also intervened in several state cases, including Boulder’s, to try to block them from moving forward. Many scientists whose work is being cited in the cases\nhave been the subject\nof more than 100 public records requests from conservative groups seeking information about their work and funders, according to Politico.\nThe first question that arose at Monday’s hearing, posed by Justice Clarence Thomas, is whether the court has jurisdiction to hear the case at this stage because no ruling has been issued.\nThe oil companies contend that the proliferating lawsuits are already causing sufficient harm and that the court should decide now whether the cases should proceed. Several justices suggested they thought it might be too early to intervene.\n“I think Boulder’s best shot is on the jurisdictional question, whether this case is too early,” Michael Gerrard, faculty director at the Sabin Center for Climate Change Law at Columbia University, said in an interview.\nThe justices also probed the arguments of whether federal law preempts a state claim about climate change and emissions, either through the Clean Air Act or the Constitution.\nSeveral of the conservative justices seemed inclined to agree that federal law preempts at least some of Boulder’s claims, Gerrard said.\nThe oil companies’ lawyers have argued that the Clean Air Act preempts any claims against oil companies tied to climate pollution. They also argue that the Constitution itself gives the federal government exclusive control over interstate pollution.\nRussell argued that Boulder’s claim does not seek to limit pollution, and that there is nothing in the Clean Air Act or the Constitution that would bar states from taking actions that have a secondary effect of reducing pollution, such as imposing a gas tax or, in this case, seeking damages from oil companies.\nJustice Samuel Alito was not at the hearing after\nrecusing himself last week\n. While Alito gave no explanation for the decision, he had been under pressure to do so because he owns stock in oil companies that are party to other lawsuits that would be affected by the court’s decision.\nAlito’s absence leaves open the possibility of a tie vote, which would allow the case to move forward.\nSupporters of the case held rallies outside the court, calling on the justices to let it proceed.\n“Boulder, like other cities and counties across the country, is confronting realities associated with climate change every day, from wildfires and floods to drought, extreme heat, and growing pressures on our water and infrastructure,” said Boulder’s city manager, Nuria Rivera-Vandermyde, in a statement after the hearing. “The legal question before the court is whether taxpayers must bear these costs alone or must the defendants also pay their fair share for the harms they have caused.”\nMany conservative groups and politicians have filed briefs supporting the oil companies, and the Trump administration presented arguments to the justices on Monday.\nBoulder won supportive briefs from not only climate advocacy groups and Democrats but also many academics and former Environmental Protection Agency officials, including some appointed by Republican presidents.\nMany of the cases brought by states and local governments have been paused pending the outcome of the Supreme Court’s ruling in the Boulder case. The decision, expected by the end of the court’s term in June, could bring those claims to an abrupt halt or set the stage for trials to finally begin.\nNicholas Kusnetz is a reporter for Inside Climate News. Before joining ICN, he worked at the Center for Public Integrity and ProPublica. His work has won numerous awards and citations, including from the Society of Professional Journalists, the Society of Environmental Journalists, the Overseas Press Club, the Society of American Business Editors and Writers, and others. His articles have appeared in more than a dozen publications, including Wired, The Washington Post, Businessweek, The Nation, and The New York Times. Nicholas can be reached on Signal at nkusnetz.15.\nThis story originally appeared on\nInside Climate News\n.\nInside Climate News\nInside Climate News\n52 Comments", "fetched_at": "2026-10-06T16:54:39.032413", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "The", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.325, "sm": 100, "em": 325, "cs": 0, "ce": 3, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "US", "s": 0.325, "e": 0.475, "sm": 325, "em": 475, "cs": 4, "ce": 6, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "Supreme", "s": 0.475, "e": 1.0, "sm": 475, "em": 999, "cs": 7, "ce": 14, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "Court", "s": 1.0, "e": 1.375, "sm": 999, "em": 1375, "cs": 15, "ce": 20, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "heard", "s": 1.387, "e": 1.65, "sm": 1387, "em": 1650, "cs": 21, "ce": 26, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "arguments", "s": 1.663, "e": 2.213, "sm": 1662, "em": 2212, "cs": 27, "ce": 36, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "from", "s": 2.225, "e": 2.4, "sm": 2225, "em": 2400, "cs": 37, "ce": 41, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "oil", "s": 2.413, "e": 2.7, "sm": 2412, "em": 2700, "cs": 42, "ce": 45, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "companies", "s": 2.712, "e": 3.2, "sm": 2712, "em": 3199, "cs": 46, "ce": 55, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "on", "s": 3.212, "e": 3.337, "sm": 3212, "em": 3337, "cs": 56, "ce": 58, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": "Monday", "s": 3.35, "e": 3.888, "sm": 3350, "em": 3887, "cs": 59, "ce": 65, "si": 0}, {"i": 11, "w": ".", "s": 3.888, "e": 4.763, "sm": 3887, "em": 4762, "cs": 65, "ce": 66, "si": 0}, {"i": 12, "w": "The", "s": 4.763, "e": 4.938, "sm": 4762, "em": 4937, "cs": 67, "ce": 70, "si": 1}, {"i": 13, "w": "case", "s": 4.95, "e": 5.275, "sm": 4950, "em": 5275, "cs": 71, "